fact WorkItem(id: "WI-803", description: some(value: "Make destructuring bind by LABEL, then drop the ORDER interim from data-tuple subtyping. THIS IS THE REAL WI-788, which WI-788 itself did not fix. The pre-WI-788 spec had already located it: \"Permutation and width are subtyping rules for data tuples whose components are read by NAME ... (A data tuple read by DESTRUCTURING is also positional; aligning such a reader by name is a known open defect, tracked as WI-788).\" The RELATION was right; the READER is wrong. WI-788 changed the relation instead and rewrote that spec paragraph to match, which refused correct programs; WI-804 restored name-keyed width and recorded the remaining order requirement as an explicit INTERIM. WHY THE INTERIM HOLDS, measured both ways: a destructuring binder list reads by SLOT and COUNT, so it is unsound under ANY subtyping step, but the failure modes differ -- WIDTH changes the count so the match fails LOUDLY, while PERMUTATION keeps the count and swaps the VALUES, silently binding a component the typer typed from a different field (an operation declared -> Int64 returning a String on a clean load). So width is admitted and order is held. THE FIX: the typer already knows the expected named-tuple type where it types a tuple pattern, so it should record WHICH LABEL each binder takes, and the matcher should fetch by label instead of by slot. PLUMBING ALREADY EXISTS: Pattern::Tuple (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~853) already carries a \"named\" vector of (Symbol, Rc<NodeOccurrence>) pairs alongside \"positional\", and it is currently DEAD -- match_tuple_pattern (eval/pattern.rs ~147) debug_asserts it empty because no producer mints a labelled tuple sub-pattern. A typer that resolves labels would move the sub-patterns from \"positional\" into \"named\", and the matcher would gain a real by-label arm in place of that refusal. NOTE the existing warning at that site: a label rule must agree with field_access short-name lookup (eval/builtins.rs), not raw symbol identity. Untyped destructuring keeps the positional path. This also makes WIDTH safe for the destructuring reader (a by-label fetch does not care about the component count), retiring the loud-trap half too. ACCEPTANCE: the order-preserving scan in align_named_tuple_fields data arm becomes an unordered name-keyed lookup and the interim notes in kernel-language.md 4.5, proposal 004 rule 4a and CLAUDE.md are removed; the WI-788 repro -- operation ap taking f typed Function with A = (a: Int64, b: String) and B = Int64, body f applied to the permuted literal (b: \"ess\", a: 3), driven by ap applied to a two-binder lambda returning its first binder -- binds the first binder to the Int64 component and returns 3; the permuted_* tests in wi788_tuple_component_order_identity_test invert to expect success; the wi782 and wi488 tests WI-788 inverted are restored to their original assertions; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-804"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T22:50:27Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-803", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Both halves control-tested independently: disabling the reader fails 8 tests, reverting the relation fails 9.\n\nRELATION: TupleAlign gained a third axis TupleOrder, exactly as the existing code comment prescribed (branch on mode.order(), never on mode == DATA). DATA=Free -- width from anywhere AND permutation; PARAM_LIST/EQUALITY stay Preserved. Free means FIRST match, which is field_access' own rule, so relation and reader stop disagreeing on a duplicate label.\n\nREADER: bind_and_label_pattern (ex extend_env_from_pattern) records each binder's component name into Pattern::Tuple.labels; match_tuple_pattern fetches via TupleComponents::by_label, one owner shared with field_access. Headline repro returns 3.\n\nDEVIATED FROM THE TICKET'S REPRESENTATION. It said move sub-patterns into the dead `named` half; that silently empties every positional-only reader (binder_arity, lambda_binder_slots, destructure_bindings, the walkers, the printer). Used a parallel `labels` sibling, STRUCTURAL rather than a RefCell annotation, so the compiler surfaced all six readers instead of letting them ignore it.\n\nMY 15 TARGETED TESTS PASSED WHILE THE CHANGE WAS BROKEN. A spread call (f(3,10) -- so foldLeft and every two-binder callback) gathers into a POSITIONAL carrier while the expected type is named, so a by-label read raised MatchFailed. Only the FULL suite caught it (wi784/wi793/wi794/wi605). Fixed with an is_name_keyed() gate: a positional carrier has no names, and reading it by slot is EXACT, not a fallback.\n\n/code-review FOUND 3 DEFECTS IN MY FIRST CUT, all fixed here: (a) by_label normalized the COMPONENT side but passed the RAW label to the _N reader -- asserting a premise on one branch and relying on its negation on the other; (b) NO double-cover guard, so duplicate component names served one component to two binders -- driven, an operation declared -> String returned Int(3); match_constructor_pattern has guarded exactly this since WI-445; (c) synthetic _1.._n labels routed into the by-label arm, which cannot resolve them against a name-keyed value. (a) and (c) are DEFENSIVE: the end-to-end fixtures I wrote for them were CONTROL-RUN and PASSED -- blind -- so they were deleted rather than kept for appearances, and the guards are pinned at the reader in wi803_by_label_reader, which does discriminate. (b)'s first fixture was also blind (it drove `-> p`, which the buggy matcher satisfies); only the SECOND binder observes the collision.\n\nKNOWN GAP, recorded at the causing site rather than spun off: pattern_to_term DROPS labels, because the reflect surface (entity tuple_pattern(elements: List[Pattern])) has nowhere to put them -- so a typed pattern round-tripped through a term silently reverts to slot reading, the WI-788 wrong answer. Instrumented: that arm is reached ZERO times across the workspace suite, for ALL tuple lowerings, so the hazard is real, has no live witness, an assert would never fire, and a reflect-surface change would ship with no coverage either.\n\nINVERTED, each kept beside the defect it came from: wi788's four permuted_* tests, wi782's permuted tuple parameter (restored to still_applies), wi488's reordered-component escape (restored to its pre-WI-788 assertion -- while it asserted the mismatch, the gate's by-name alignment had NO coverage at all), and wi800's two. A speculative test I added asserting unification still refuses a permutation was REMOVED, not weakened: List[T = ...] goes through subtyping, so it proved nothing; EQUALITY is pinned directly at the relation instead.\n\nWI-805's program is now REFUSED as a side effect -- recorded, not claimed. Its own fix (refuse the duplicate where the tuple is BUILT) is still open, and a same-typed duplicate still loads.\n\nDOCS reconciled: kernel-language.md 4.5 / Arrow types / \"Destructuring binds by LABEL\", proposal 004 rule 4a, CLAUDE.md. /simplify also caught that I added a third axis while leaving SIX comments saying \"two axes\" plus a grid test blind to the new axis -- the exact drift WI-799's own comment names.\n\nFull workspace 3341 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. Committed 5a0970ad.", at: "2026-07-21T22:50:53Z")
